# Database Guidelines

## When to Check These Rules
- When creating or modifying database schemas
- When writing database queries
- When implementing database optimizations
- When handling database migrations
- When working with ActiveRecord models

## Migrations
- Always use the Rails Rake task to generate migrations
- Never manually create migration files
- Use the format: `bin/rails generate migration [MigrationName] [column:type]`
- Let Rails handle the timestamp and migration number
- Example: `bin/rails generate migration AddNameToUsers name:string`
- Include proper indexes for foreign keys
- Use reversible migrations when possible
- Add appropriate constraints (e.g., null: false, default: value)
- Document complex migrations with comments

## Query Optimization
- Use includes for eager loading
- Avoid N+1 queries
- Follow ActiveRecord best practices
- Use appropriate indexes
- Optimize complex queries
- Use counter caches when needed
- Consider using database views for complex queries
- Implement proper database constraints
- Use database transactions for atomic operations

## Examples

✅ Correct:
```ruby
# Proper eager loading
User.includes(:posts).where(active: true)

# Using counter cache
class Post < ApplicationRecord
  belongs_to :user, counter_cache: true
end

# Proper migration
class AddNameToUsers < ActiveRecord::Migration[7.0]
  def change
    add_column :users, :name, :string, null: false
    add_index :users, :name
  end
end
```

❌ Incorrect:
- Manual migration file creation
- Missing indexes on foreign keys
- N+1 queries
- No eager loading
- Missing database constraints
- Improper transaction usage

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Not using eager loading
- Creating N+1 queries
- Missing database indexes
- Not using counter caches
- Improper migration structure
- Missing database constraints
- Not using transactions when needed
- Poor query optimization

## Validation Checklist
- [ ] Migrations follow Rails conventions
- [ ] Proper indexes are in place
- [ ] N+1 queries are eliminated
- [ ] Eager loading is used appropriately
- [ ] Counter caches are implemented where needed
- [ ] Database constraints are properly set
- [ ] Complex queries are optimized
- [ ] Transactions are used for atomic operations
